@NODE MAIN "Ultimate Amiga OS — Documentation Index"
@TITLE "Ultimate Amiga OS (UAOS) Help System"

  @{b}Ultimate Amiga OS (UAOS)@{ub}
  @{i}A bare-metal 64-bit OS for x86_64 with transparent M68k emulation@{ui}

  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  Welcome to the UAOS help system.  Use the buttons below or your
  AmigaGuide viewer's navigation keys to browse the documentation.

  @{" Quickstart              " LINK QUICKSTART}
     Get UAOS running in under five minutes.

  @{" Hardware Configuration  " LINK HARDWARE}
     BIOS/UEFI settings, GRUB boot parameters, memory layout.

  @{" AmigaDOS Integration    " LINK AMIGADOS}
     AmigaDOS command paths, assignments, and POSIX namespace bridge.

  @{" M68k Emulation Layer    " LINK EMULATION}
     Thunk dispatch, ROM patches, and custom chip register interception.

  @{" Kernel Subsystems       " LINK KERNEL}
     Exec, MMU sandbox, page fault handler, ROM module registry.

  @{" TCP/IP Networking       " LINK NETWORKING}
     Network drivers, stack init, ping, UDP, TCP, DHCP.

  @{" Building from Source    " LINK BUILDING}
     Cross-compiler toolchain setup and ISO packaging pipeline.

  @{" Troubleshooting         " LINK TROUBLE}
     Common problems and diagnostic procedures.

@ENDNODE


@NODE QUICKSTART "Quickstart Guide"
@TITLE "UAOS — Quickstart"
@PREV MAIN
@NEXT HARDWARE

  @{b}Quickstart@{ub}
  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  @{b}Minimum Requirements@{ub}

    • x86_64 CPU with 64-bit long mode and PAE support
    • 512 MB RAM (2 GB recommended)
    • BIOS or UEFI firmware with legacy boot or GRUB2 support
    • CD-ROM drive or USB port for ISO boot media

  @{b}Booting the ISO@{ub}

  1. Write @{i}build/Ultimate_Amiga_OS.iso@{ui} to a USB drive or burn to CD-R:

       dd if=Ultimate_Amiga_OS.iso of=/dev/sdX bs=4M status=progress

  2. Set your BIOS/UEFI boot order to boot from the removable medium.

  3. At the GRUB menu select @{b}Ultimate Amiga OS (UAOS) — Normal Boot@{ub}.
     The kernel loads, initialises the MMU sandbox, registers Exec ROM
     modules, and launches the M68k emulator run-loop.

  @{b}Testing in QEMU@{ub}

       qemu-system-x86_64 -cdrom build/Ultimate_Amiga_OS.iso \
           -m 512M -boot d -vga std

  @{b}First Steps@{ub}

    • The AmigaDOS CLI is accessible via @{b}SYS:C/Shell@{ub}.
    • GNU tools are merged into @{b}C:@{ub} — type @{i}ls@{ui}, @{i}gcc@{ui}, @{i}make@{ui} as normal.
    • The Workbench screen can be opened with @{i}LoadWB@{ui}.

  @{"  Back to Index  " LINK MAIN}

@ENDNODE


@NODE HARDWARE "Hardware Configuration"
@TITLE "UAOS — Hardware Configuration"
@PREV QUICKSTART
@NEXT AMIGADOS

  @{b}Hardware Configuration@{ub}
  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  @{b}BIOS / UEFI Settings@{ub}

    • Disable Secure Boot (UAOS kernel is not signed by default).
    • Enable Legacy USB Support if booting from USB on older boards.
    • Set memory above 4 GB to @{i}above 4G MMIO@{ui} if available.

  @{b}GRUB Boot Parameters@{ub}

  Pass on the kernel command line (edit grub.cfg or press @{b}e@{ub} at menu):

    @{b}sysroot=@{ub}/boot/uaos-sysroot.img   Path to the sysroot archive module.
    @{b}config=@{ub}/boot/aros_kickstart.conf  Path to the AROS ROM configuration.
    @{b}loglevel=@{ub}[debug|info|warn|error]  Kernel log verbosity.
    @{b}vga=@{ub}0x0318                        VESA mode 1024×768 32 bpp.
    @{b}console=@{ub}ttyS0,115200              Redirect console to serial port.

  @{b}Memory Address Map@{ub}

    0x00000000 – 0x001FFFFF   Chip RAM  (2 MB)
    0x00200000 – 0x00FFFFFF   Fast RAM  (14 MB lower window)
    0x00B00000 – 0x00DFFFFF   @{b}Custom chip / CIA registers@{ub} (MMU-trapped)
    0x00F80000 – 0x00FFFFFF   Kickstart ROM mirror
    0x01000000 – 0x7FFFFFFF   Extended Fast RAM (up to 2 GB)
    0x80000000 – 0xFFFFFFFF   x86_64 kernel and driver space

  The address window 0x00B00000–0x00DFFFFF is intentionally left
  non-present in the paging tables.  Any access triggers a #PF which
  is intercepted by the UAOS page fault handler and forwarded to the
  custom chip emulator.  See @{"Kernel Subsystems" LINK KERNEL}.

  @{b}Supported Video Hardware@{ub}

    • VESA/VBE 2.0+ framebuffer (universal fallback)
    • UEFI GOP framebuffer
    • VirtIO-GPU (for QEMU/KVM guests)

  @{b}Supported Network Hardware@{ub}

    • Intel 82540EM (e1000)  — VirtualBox Intel PRO/1000 MT Desktop
    • VirtIO-Net             — QEMU -device virtio-net-pci,disable-modern=on

  See @{"TCP/IP Networking" LINK NETWORKING} for driver setup and API.

  @{b}Supported Audio Hardware@{ub}

    • SoundBlaster 16 (ISA/PCI)
    • Intel HD Audio (HDA)

  @{"  Back to Index  " LINK MAIN}

@ENDNODE


@NODE AMIGADOS "AmigaDOS Integration"
@TITLE "UAOS — AmigaDOS Integration"
@PREV HARDWARE
@NEXT EMULATION

  @{b}AmigaDOS Integration@{ub}
  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  UAOS presents a merged AmigaDOS / POSIX namespace.  AmigaDOS assigns
  map onto the following host directories:

    @{b}SYS:@{ub}    →  sys-root/            Boot volume root (AmigaOS semantics)
    @{b}C:@{ub}      →  sys-root/C/        AmigaDOS + GNU binary path
    @{b}S:@{ub}      →  sys-root/S/        Startup-Sequence and scripts
    @{b}L:@{ub}      →  sys-root/L/        Filesystem handler libraries
    @{b}DEVS:@{ub}   →  sys-root/DEVS/     Device and MountList entries

  @{b}Startup-Sequence@{ub}

  The file @{b}S:Startup-Sequence@{ub} is executed by AmigaDOS immediately after
  Exec initialises.  It sets up assigns, mounts devices, and optionally
  launches the Workbench or a CLI shell.

  @{b}GNU Tool Integration@{ub}

  GNU binaries (bash, gcc, make, ls, grep, etc.) are installed into
  @{b}C:@{ub} alongside native AmigaDOS commands.  UAOS provides a thin POSIX
  syscall shim that maps Unix file descriptors and paths onto the
  AmigaDOS I/O model.

  @{b}AmigaDOS Path Syntax@{ub}

    Absolute:   SYS:Utilities/More
    Relative:   :Utilities/More        (from current volume)
    Parent:     /Utilities/More        (one directory up)

  @{b}Useful Commands@{ub}

    @{i}List SYS:@{ui}             Directory listing of SYS:
    @{i}Assign@{ui}                Show all current assignments
    @{i}Mount DEVS:DOSDrivers/@{ui} Mount all configured devices
    @{i}LoadWB@{ui}                Launch the Workbench GUI

  @{"  Back to Index  " LINK MAIN}

@ENDNODE


@NODE EMULATION "M68k Emulation Layer"
@TITLE "UAOS — M68k Emulation Layer"
@PREV AMIGADOS
@NEXT KERNEL

  @{b}M68k Emulation Layer@{ub}
  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  UAOS runs a headless M68k emulator core (derived from UAE/FS-UAE) inside
  a 4 GB virtual address sandbox.  Amiga software executes unmodified at
  near-native speed via the JIT recompiler.

  @{b}Thunk Dispatch Mechanism@{ub}

  Every intercepted Exec library call is replaced in the AROS ROM with a
  6-byte breakout sequence assembled by the @{b}MK_THUNK@{ub} macro in
  @{i}emulation/rom_patches/rom_traps.s@{ui}:

      0x4AFC   ILLEGAL opcode  — triggers the JIT trap callback
      0x414D   UAOS signature  — "AM", validates the trap origin
      0xNNNN   Function index  — identifies the library call

  When the emulator decodes @{b}0x4AFC@{ub}, it invokes @{b}UAOS_Bridge_IllegalOpcode()@{ub}
  which calls @{b}UAOS_HandleThunk()@{ub}.  The thunk handler:

    1. Reads the signature word and function index from guest memory.
    2. Validates the @{b}0x414D@{ub} signature.
    3. Extracts arguments from M68k registers per Amiga ABI conventions
       (A1 = pointer args, D0/D1 = integer args).
    4. Calls the native x86_64 C stub.
    5. Writes return values back into D0/A0.
    6. Advances the guest PC by 6 bytes past the breakout sequence.

  @{b}ROM Patch Configuration@{ub}

  @{i}emulation/rom_patches/aros_kickstart.conf@{ui} controls which ROM image is
  loaded and which Exec vectors are intercepted.  Set
  @{b}thunk_trace = yes@{ub} to log every intercepted call to stderr.

  @{b}Custom Chip Register Access@{ub}

  The address range 0x00B00000–0x00DFFFFF is unmapped in the MMU sandbox.
  Reads and writes from the emulated M68k to this range fault through to
  the page fault handler which forwards them to the chip emulator.
  See @{"Kernel Subsystems" LINK KERNEL}.

  @{"  Back to Index  " LINK MAIN}

@ENDNODE


@NODE KERNEL "Kernel Subsystems"
@TITLE "UAOS — Kernel Subsystems"
@PREV EMULATION
@NEXT NETWORKING

  @{b}Kernel Subsystems@{ub}
  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  @{b}MMU Sandbox (kernel/exec/mmu_sandbox.c)@{ub}

  @{b}UAOS_MMU_Init()@{ub} constructs 4-level paging tables (PML4 → PDPT → PD)
  mapping the entire 4 GB guest address space using 2 MB huge pages.
  Pages covering 0x00B00000–0x00DFFFFF have @{b}PAGE_PRESENT@{ub} cleared and
  @{b}PAGE_NO_CACHE@{ub} set, making them invisible to normal loads and stores
  but identifiable by the fault handler.  The completed PML4 is loaded
  into @{b}CR3@{ub} to activate the sandbox.

  @{b}Page Fault Handler (kernel/exec/page_fault_handler.c)@{ub}

  @{b}uaos_page_fault_isr@{ub} is registered at IDT vector 14.  On entry it saves
  all 15 general-purpose registers and calls @{b}UAOS_PageFaultHandler()@{ub}:

    • Reads @{b}CR2@{ub} for the fault address.
    • If outside the chip window, halts (unrecoverable fault).
    • @{b}Write fault:@{ub} forwards RAX + chip offset to @{b}chip_emu_write()@{ub}.
    • @{b}Read fault:@{ub}  decodes the faulting MOV instruction's ModRM byte,
      queries @{b}chip_emu_read()@{ub}, injects the result into the destination
      register slot on the interrupt stack, then advances RIP past the
      faulting instruction before executing @{b}IRETQ@{ub}.

  @{b}ROM Module Registry (kernel/exec/rom_modules.c)@{ub}

  @{b}UAOS_ROM_RegisterAll()@{ub} populates the module registry at boot time.
  Each module descriptor holds the library name, version, 32-bit Amiga
  base address, and an array of native function pointers.
  @{b}UAOS_ROM_NativeFunc()@{ub} resolves a (library name, function index) pair
  to the matching host function pointer for the thunk dispatcher.

  @{b}Thunk Handler (kernel/exec/thunk_handler.c)@{ub}

  See @{"M68k Emulation Layer" LINK EMULATION} for the full dispatch flow.
  The @{b}UAOS_AMIGA_TO_HOST()@{ub} macro translates any 32-bit Amiga guest address
  to the corresponding host linear address relative to the RAM base pointer
  set by @{b}UAOS_SetRamBase()@{ub}.

  @{"  Back to Index  " LINK MAIN}

@ENDNODE


@NODE NETWORKING "TCP/IP Networking"
@TITLE "UAOS — TCP/IP Networking"
@PREV KERNEL
@NEXT BUILDING

  @{b}TCP/IP Networking@{ub}
  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  UAOS includes a complete freestanding TCP/IP stack.  It auto-detects the
  network card at boot, runs DHCP, and falls back to a static IP if DHCP
  times out.  The stack runs in the kernel event loop — no threads needed.

  @{b}Supported Hardware@{ub}

    • @{b}Intel 82540EM (e1000)@{ub}  — VirtualBox (Intel PRO/1000 MT Desktop),
      QEMU @{i}-device e1000@{ui}.  PCI vendor 0x8086 / device 0x100E.
    • @{b}VirtIO-Net@{ub}             — QEMU @{i}-device virtio-net-pci,disable-modern=on@{ui}.
      PCI vendor 0x1AF4 / device 0x1000.

  The correct driver is selected automatically via @{b}netdev_probe()@{ui}.
  e1000 is always tried first; virtio-net is the fallback.

  @{b}Stack Layers@{ub}

    @{b}kernel/net/stack.c@{ub}   — top-level init, DHCP orchestration, poll/tick
    @{b}kernel/net/ip.c@{ub}      — IPv4 send/receive, routing via gateway
    @{b}kernel/net/arp.c@{ub}     — ARP request/reply, 16-entry cache
    @{b}kernel/net/icmp.c@{ub}    — echo request/reply (ping)
    @{b}kernel/net/udp.c@{ub}     — UDP sockets (up to 8, 2 KB RX each)
    @{b}kernel/net/tcp.c@{ub}     — TCP sockets (up to 8, 4 KB TX/RX each)
    @{b}kernel/net/dhcp.c@{ub}    — DHCP DISCOVER/REQUEST/ACK over UDP

  @{b}Initialisation@{ub}

  Called once from @{b}uaos_kernel_main()@{ub}:

      net_stack_init(IPV4(10,0,2,15), IPV4(10,0,2,2), IPV4(255,255,255,0));

  Or with an explicit DHCP timeout (milliseconds):

      net_stack_init_ex(fallback_ip, fallback_gw, fallback_nm, 2000);

  @{b}Key API Functions@{ub}

    @{b}Stack (stack.h)@{ub}
      @{i}net_stack_is_up()@{ui}        — returns 1 if NIC found and configured
      @{i}net_stack_poll()@{ui}         — process pending RX frames (call from loop)
      @{i}net_stack_tick()@{ui}         — TCP retransmit timer (call from PIT IRQ)
      @{i}net_stack_get_ip()@{ui}       — returns assigned IPv4 as ipv4_t
      @{i}net_ip_to_str(ip, buf)@{ui}   — format IP to "a.b.c.d" string
      @{i}net_str_to_ip(str, out)@{ui}  — parse "a.b.c.d" to ipv4_t

    @{b}ICMP (icmp.h)@{ub}
      @{i}icmp_ping(dst, seq)@{ui}      — send one ICMP echo request
      @{i}icmp_got_reply()@{ui}         — returns 1 when echo reply arrives
      @{i}icmp_clear_reply()@{ui}       — reset reply flag before each ping

    @{b}UDP (udp.h)@{ub}
      @{i}udp_open(local_port)@{ui}     — bind socket; 0 = ephemeral port
      @{i}udp_send(s,ip,port,buf,len)@{ui} — send datagram
      @{i}udp_recv(s,buf,max,&ip,&p)@{ui} — non-blocking receive; 0 if empty
      @{i}udp_close(s)@{ui}             — release socket

    @{b}TCP (tcp.h)@{ub}
      @{i}tcp_connect(ip,port,lport)@{ui} — active open; returns socket or -1
      @{i}tcp_listen(port)@{ui}          — passive open; returns socket or -1
      @{i}tcp_accept(lsock)@{ui}         — accept pending connection
      @{i}tcp_send(s,data,len)@{ui}      — queue data for sending
      @{i}tcp_recv(s,buf,max)@{ui}       — non-blocking receive
      @{i}tcp_close(s)@{ui}              — graceful close (sends FIN)
      @{i}tcp_state(s)@{ui}              — returns TcpState enum value

    @{b}ARP (arp.h)@{ub}
      @{i}arp_lookup(ip, mac_out)@{ui}   — 1 if found in cache, 0 if miss
      @{i}arp_request(ip)@{ui}           — broadcast ARP who-has
      @{i}arp_cache_update(ip, mac)@{ui} — insert/refresh cache entry

  @{b}Shell Commands@{ub}

    @{i}ping <ip> [count]@{ui}   — ICMP echo; default 4 packets, 1-second timeout each
    @{i}ifconfig@{ui}             — show IP, netmask, gateway, MAC, DHCP/static flag

  @{b}Polling Model@{ub}

  No separate network thread exists.  Frames are processed either:
    • from the NIC's IRQ handler (calls @{i}netdev_poll()@{ui} inline), or
    • from @{i}net_stack_poll()@{ui} called by the main @{i}hlt@{ui}-loop dispatcher.
  Both paths share a reentrancy lock so simultaneous calls are safe.

  @{b}VirtualBox Quick-Start@{ub}

  1. VM Settings → Network → Adapter 1
  2. Attached to: @{b}NAT@{ub}, Adapter type: @{b}Intel PRO/1000 MT Desktop@{ub}
  3. Boot UAOS — e1000 detected automatically, DHCP runs.
  4. @{i}ping 10.0.2.2@{ui} — gateway reachable.
  5. @{i}ping 8.8.8.8@{ui}  — full internet via VirtualBox NAT.

  @{b}QEMU Quick-Start@{ub}

      qemu-system-x86_64 ... \
          -netdev user,id=n0 \
          -device virtio-net-pci,netdev=n0,disable-modern=on

  @{i}ping 10.0.2.2@{ui} works.  Pings to external IPs require host @{b}CAP_NET_RAW@{ub};
  TCP and UDP are fully proxied by slirp regardless.

  @{"  Back to Index  " LINK MAIN}

@ENDNODE


@NODE BUILDING "Building from Source"
@TITLE "UAOS — Building from Source"
@PREV NETWORKING
@NEXT TROUBLE

  @{b}Building from Source@{ub}
  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  @{b}Prerequisites@{ub}

    • GCC cross-compiler targeting x86_64-elf (freestanding)
    • GNU Binutils (ld, objcopy, nasm/gas)
    • m68k-elf-as  (for rom_traps.s)
    • GRUB2 tools: grub-mkrescue, grub-pc-bin
    • xorriso (ISO 9660 image writer)
    • Python 3.8+  (mock kernel generator in build_iso.sh)

  On Debian/Ubuntu:

      sudo apt install gcc-multilib binutils xorriso \
          grub-pc-bin grub-common \
          binutils-m68k-linux-gnu python3

  @{b}Repository Layout@{ub}

      emulation/         M68k emulator bridge and ROM patches
      kernel/exec/       Core kernel subsystems
      kernel/dos/        AmigaDOS namespace layer (in progress)
      kernel/intuition/  Intuition UI engine (in progress)
      drivers/           Audio, video, network drivers
      scripts/           Build and packaging scripts
      sys-root/          Live AmigaDOS filesystem tree
      documentation/     This help system
      build/             Generated output (created by build_iso.sh)

  @{b}Building the ISO@{ub}

      chmod +x scripts/build_iso.sh
      ./scripts/build_iso.sh

  The script stages all artefacts, generates a mock multiboot2 kernel
  binary, and invokes grub-mkrescue.  Output: @{b}build/Ultimate_Amiga_OS.iso@{ub}.

  For a clean rebuild:

      ./scripts/build_iso.sh --clean

  @{b}Testing@{ub}

      qemu-system-x86_64 -cdrom build/Ultimate_Amiga_OS.iso \
          -m 512M -boot d -vga std -serial stdio

  @{"  Back to Index  " LINK MAIN}

@ENDNODE


@NODE TROUBLE "Troubleshooting"
@TITLE "UAOS — Troubleshooting"
@PREV BUILDING
@NEXT MAIN

  @{b}Troubleshooting@{ub}
  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  @{b}build_iso.sh fails: grub-mkrescue not found@{ub}

      sudo apt install grub-pc-bin grub-common xorriso

  @{b}QEMU: No bootable device@{ub}

    Ensure the ISO was produced without errors.  Verify it contains a
    GRUB EFI/BIOS boot record:

        file build/Ultimate_Amiga_OS.iso
        isoinfo -d -i build/Ultimate_Amiga_OS.iso | grep -i bootable

  @{b}Thunk handler: RAM base not set@{ub}

    Call @{b}UAOS_SetRamBase()@{ub} (or @{b}UAOS_Bridge_Init()@{ub}) before starting the
    emulator run-loop.  The bridge init function allocates the 4 GB window
    and calls SetRamBase automatically.

  @{b}Page fault outside chip window — kernel halts@{ub}

    A #PF occurred at an address outside 0x00B00000–0x00DFFFFF.  This
    indicates either a genuine null-pointer dereference in the kernel or
    a missing page table entry.  Boot with @{b}loglevel=debug@{ub} and redirect
    output to serial:

        console=ttyS0,115200

    then inspect the fault address reported before the halt.

  @{b}Unknown thunk function index@{ub}

    The rom_traps.s function index does not have a matching case in
    UAOS_HandleThunk().  Add the stub and a new @{b}#define THUNK_@{ub}* constant
    in thunk_handler.c, and add the corresponding entry to rom_traps.s.

  @{b}ILLEGAL opcode crash in guest — not a UAOS trap@{ub}

    The 0x414D signature word was not present after 0x4AFC.  The guest
    has executed a genuine illegal instruction.  Enable
    @{b}break_on_illegal = yes@{ub} in aros_kickstart.conf to trap it in the
    debugger and inspect the guest PC and surrounding memory.

  @{"  Back to Index  " LINK MAIN}

@ENDNODE
